{"name":"Saturnia","hex":"#dddbce","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Saturnia","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A taxonomic genus within the family Saturniidae – certain emperor moths."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A spa town in Tuscany, north-central Italy, that has been inhabited since ancient times."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Saturnia"}],"entries":[{"title":"Saturnia","blocks":[{"text":"Saturnia is a spa town in Tuscany in central Italy that has been inhabited since ancient times. It is a frazione of the comune of Manciano, in the province of Grosseto. Famous for the spa which gives it its name, its population is 280."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Saturnia_Cascate_del_Mulino.jpg/330px-Saturnia_Cascate_del_Mulino.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saturnia","wikidata":"Q1511221"},{"title":"Saturnia (moth)","blocks":[{"text":"Saturnia is a genus of large silkmoths in the family Saturniidae, which the German biologist Franz von Paula Schrank first described in 1802. Its members are commonly named emperor moths, though this is also used for various close relatives in subfamily Saturniinae. The known species are found across the Palearctic. However, three allied species, commonly called \"saturnia moths\" inhabit the chaparral of California. These have been included as species of the genus Saturnia in some schemes, but recently are treated as a separate genus Calosaturnia, only in the United States."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Saturnia_pyri.jpg/330px-Saturnia_pyri.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saturnia_(moth)","wikidata":"Q1325847"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Saturnia%2C_chiesa_di_santa_maria_maddalena%2C_Madon